---
title: "Arnab Chakraborty"
description: "Arnab Chakraborty — Co-founder & CTO of Scalefusion. Writing on scale, operations and software security."
---

I am the Co-founder & CTO of **[ProMobi Technologies](https://promobitech.com "ProMobi Technologies")**. We make **[Scalefusion](https://www.scalefusion.com "Scalefusion")**, an enterprise device management platform used by more than 12,000 customers across 120+ countries.

I am an autodidact and learnt most of my programming skills by myself. I value and admire quality code, clean user interfaces and beautiful typography. These days I mostly work on scalability, DevOps, software security and mentoring team members.

> I have one rule I keep coming back to: if you have to read the manual to use the software, it was not made intuitive enough. That applies to interfaces, to APIs, and to the codebases and processes engineers have to live inside.

## Building the company

What began as a small product is now a company of 300+ people, selling through 200+ partners worldwide. Most of what I have done over the last decade does not look like programming: hiring engineers, building teams from the ground up, setting the culture and the processes those teams work inside, and deciding which of them to keep as the company outgrew them.

The part I have not stopped caring about is the software itself — that it stays fast, stays secure, and stays something the people who wrote it are willing to put their name on.

## Where the writing went

The archive here stops in 2013. That is not an oversight. It is roughly when ProMobi stopped being a side project and started being a company, and writing was the first thing I gave up. It stayed gone for more than a decade.

The old Android and JavaScript posts are still here because they are an honest record of what I was thinking about at the time, and because those URLs have been alive since 2010 and I would rather not break them. What I write next will look different — scale, operations, security, and what building a company teaches you about software that building software does not.

